@efiring Thanks for the clarification. I forgot about the more "sophisticated" plot() variant for simultaneously plotting multiple data sets. The x, y handling makes sense, even though it's unfortunately not clear from simple cases why scatter() and plot() must behave differently sometimes.
I also see that consistent handling of c is more cumbersome.
Given that, I still support @anntzer's suggestion to deprecate any non-1D input to scatter(). There's little benefit in auto-flatting. Removing that would make a more consistent API: no differences between x, y, c handling. And, while plot() and scatter() won't be the same, they become more similar making it easier to guess the behavior of one knowning the other.

Updated the issue title to be a bit clearer about where the validation is being done.
@timhoffm @anntzer Seems reasonable to move to eager color evaluation. This also would also move us a long way towards "always store colors and RBGA float tuples internally".  On the other hand, this would mean that a.set_color(C); C != a.get_color() in general which is not great.  Not sure carrying 2 copies of the color (one 'human readable', one 'RGBA') is a better path though.
Currently, we have to resolve CN against the default cycle from rcparams because the Axes does not actually hold the underlying cycler instance, it only holds an itertools.cycle() of the cycler.  Even though it cuts off some use-cases (like having 4 axes each with their own property cycler that have different colors and then expecting 'C0' to have different values in each), it greatly simplifies both implementation and how hard it is to explain to always go against the default cycle .

We could make Color inherit from tuple, but just override __eq__ so that it converts the other argument with to_color_rgba first before comparing (possibly, also stash the arguments with which it was constructed and use it in its __str__/__repr__ as well).
So something very approximately like
class Color(tuple):
    def __new__(cls, *args, **kwargs):
        self = cls(*mcolors.to_rgba(*args, **kwargs))
        self._args, self._kwargs = args, kwargs
        return self
    def __eq__(self, other):
        return tuple(self) == mcolors.to_rgba(other)
    def __str__(self):
        return super().__str__(self) + f" (a.k.a. Color(*{self._args}, **{self._kwargs}))"

I think your plot is expected and makes sense - for angles='xy' the U/V values are interpreted as being in in a polar coordinate system too, so you're asking the arrow to point from (r, theta) to (r + u, theta + v). Since all your u/v values are 1, the arrows make sense (you can see they aren't quite pointing exactly along the theta direction, as they have a small radius component).
@johncoxon do you have suggestions as to how the documentation could be clarified? Maybe adding something like "For non-cartesian coordinates x/y are interpreted as the first and second axes." could be added somewhere?
As an aside, I think setting angles="uv" does what you expect, which is interpreting the u/v values as in the cartesian space of the display.
